{"results":[{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"Q9HD67","uniProtkbId":"MYO10_HUMAN","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Unconventional myosin-X"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"Unconventional myosin-10"}}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"value":"MYO10"},"synonyms":[{"value":"KIAA0799"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16894163"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"18570893"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"27580874"}],"value":"Myosins are actin-based motor molecules with ATPase activity. Unconventional myosins serve in intracellular movements. MYO10 binds to actin filaments and actin bundles and functions as a plus end-directed motor. Moves with higher velocity and takes larger steps on actin bundles than on single actin filaments (PubMed:27580874). The tail domain binds to membranous compartments containing phosphatidylinositol 3,4,5-trisphosphate or integrins, and mediates cargo transport along actin filaments. Regulates cell shape, cell spreading and cell adhesion. Stimulates the formation and elongation of filopodia. In hippocampal neurons it induces the formation of dendritic filopodia by trafficking the actin-remodeling protein VASP to the tips of filopodia, where it promotes actin elongation. Plays a role in formation of the podosome belt in osteoclasts"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250","source":"UniProtKB","id":"F8VQB6"}],"value":"Functions as a dominant-negative regulator of isoform 1, suppressing its filopodia-inducing and axon outgrowth-promoting activities. In hippocampal neurons, it increases VASP retention in spine heads to induce spine formation and spine head expansion (By similarity)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION","molecule":"Isoform Headless"}],"extraAttributes":{"uniParcId":"UPI0001597062"}},
{"entryType":"UniProtKB unreviewed (TrEMBL)","primaryAccession":"A0A0A0MQX1","uniProtkbId":"A0A0A0MQX1_HUMAN","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00069451"}],"value":"Unconventional myosin-X"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00078788"}],"value":"Unconventional myosin-10"}}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000313","source":"Ensembl","id":"ENSP00000274203.10"}],"value":"MYO10"}}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000256","source":"ARBA","id":"ARBA00060002"}],"value":"Myosins are actin-based motor molecules with ATPase activity. Unconventional myosins serve in intracellular movements. MYO10 binds to actin filaments and actin bundles and functions as a plus end-directed motor. Moves with higher velocity and takes larger steps on actin bundles than on single actin filaments. The tail domain binds to membranous compartments containing phosphatidylinositol 3,4,5-trisphosphate or integrins, and mediates cargo transport along actin filaments. Regulates cell shape, cell spreading and cell adhesion. Stimulates the formation and elongation of filopodia. In hippocampal neurons it induces the formation of dendritic filopodia by trafficking the actin-remodeling protein VASP to the tips of filopodia, where it promotes actin elongation. Plays a role in formation of the podosome belt in osteoclasts"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"}],"extraAttributes":{"uniParcId":"UPI0000D61603"}},
